{"record":"parent_entry","position":{"parent_record":"request:00000000000000000048:0000","subposition":null},"original_role":"tool","source":"tool_result","trust":"untrusted","provenance":null,"content":"{\"kind\":\"tool_result\",\"call_id\":\"call_01a169c824387129815b26d58f3ad66d\",\"output\":\"Read text file `CONTRIBUTING.md`.\\n1|# Contributing Guidelines\\n2|\\n3|Thanks for your interest in contributing to AI Chat WebUI.\\n4|\\n5|## Getting started\\n6|1. Fork the repository.\\n7|2. Create a branch from `main` for your change.\\n8|3. Install dependencies with `npm install`.\\n9|4. Copy `.env.example` to `.env` and adjust values if needed.\\n10|\\n11|## Development workflow\\n12|- Keep changes focused and minimal.\\n13|- Preserve existing behavior unless a fix/refactor explicitly requires change.\\n14|- Prefer configuration as the source of truth.\\n15|- Update documentation when changing behavior, setup, or configuration.\\n16|\\n17|## Code style\\n18|- Follow the current JavaScript style in the repo.\\n19|- Avoid introducing unnecessary frameworks or tooling.\\n20|- Do not commit generated artifacts or `node_modules/`.\\n21|\\n22|## Commit and pull requests\\n23|- Use clear, descriptive commit messages.\\n24|- In pull requests, include:\\n25|  - What changed\\n26|  - Why it changed\\n27|  - How it was tested\\n28|  - Any known limitations\\n29|\\n30|## Reporting issues\\n31|When opening an issue, include:\\n32|- Steps to reproduce\\n33|- Expected vs actual behavior\\n34|- Relevant logs/screenshots\\n35|- Environment details (OS, Node version, Ollama version)\\n36|\\n37|\\n38|## Binary files\\n39|- Pull requests should not include generated binary files or release artifacts.\\n40|- Remove accidental binary additions before pushing:\\n41|  ```bash\\n42|  git rm --cached <path-to-binary>\\n43|  ```\\n44|- Keep the repository focused on source code, configuration, and documentation.\\n45|- See `docs/BINARY_FILES.md` for the PR binary-file policy used by this project.\"}","truncated":false}
{"record":"parent_entry","position":{"parent_record":"request:00000000000000000049:0000","subposition":null},"original_role":"tool","source":"tool_result","trust":"untrusted","provenance":null,"content":"{\"kind\":\"tool_result\",\"call_id\":\"call_01a169c825be76748eef1cc4325f8c17\",\"output\":\"Read text file `SECURITY_REVIEW.md`.\\n1|# Security Review Findings\\n2|\\n3|## 1) Stored/Reflected XSS via unsanitized `innerHTML` (Critical)\\n4|- `thinkingMessage.querySelector('.message-text').innerHTML = data.response;` inserts model output directly into the DOM as HTML.\\n5|- `formatContent()` also transforms untrusted content into HTML without escaping first.\\n6|- Impact: any malicious model response (or upstream prompt-injection content) can execute JavaScript in the user's browser.\\n7|\\n8|## 2) Information leakage in API error responses (Medium)\\n9|- Server returns internal exception details to clients via `details: error.message` in both route-level and global handlers.\\n10|- This may expose backend URLs, upstream response details, and implementation behavior useful for attackers.\\n11|\\n12|## 3) Overly permissive CORS policy (Medium)\\n13|- API sets `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` for all requests.\\n14|- If this service is ever exposed beyond localhost or includes auth/session context later, this enables untrusted origins to read API responses cross-origin.\\n15|\\n16|## 4) License/compliance mismatch affecting GPL-3.0 distribution claims (High compliance risk)\\n17|- Project metadata declares GPL-3.0-only.\\n18|- README simultaneously states MIT licensing.\\n19|- This contradiction creates legal ambiguity for downstream users and redistributors; it must be resolved before open-sourcing.\"}","truncated":false}
{"record":"parent_entry","position":{"parent_record":"request:00000000000000000050:0000","subposition":null},"original_role":"tool","source":"tool_result","trust":"untrusted","provenance":null,"content":"{\"kind\":\"tool_result\",\"call_id\":\"call_01a169c8279574da9445ff0ccf43001e\",\"output\":\"Read text file `docs/BINARY_FILES.md`.\\n1|# Binary Files in Pull Requests\\n2|\\n3|This repository should contain source code and documentation only.\\n4|\\n5|## Why this matters\\n6|Some PR tooling used in this project rejects binary diffs with the message:\\n7|\\n8|`Binärdateien werden nicht unterstützt`.\\n9|\\n10|To keep PR creation reliable, do not commit generated binaries or release artifacts.\\n11|\\n12|## Do not commit\\n13|- Compiled binaries (`*.exe`, `*.dll`, `*.so`, `*.dylib`, `*.bin`)\\n14|- Build output folders (`dist/`, `build/`)\\n15|- Archive artifacts (`*.zip`, `*.tar`, `*.tar.gz`, `*.tgz`, `*.7z`)\\n16|\\n17|## If a binary file was added by mistake\\n18|Remove it from Git history of your branch before opening a PR:\\n19|\\n20|```bash\\n21|git rm --cached <path-to-binary>\\n22|```\\n23|\\n24|Then commit and push again.\"}","truncated":false}
